# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your Client ID, then restart the
# dev server. Without it the app runs, but the Clips footer will say Drive is
# not configured and sign-in will do nothing.
#
# The OAuth Client ID is NOT a secret — it identifies the app, it does not
# authorise anything, and it ships in the JS bundle of any static site. Google's
# docs treat it as public. It lives here rather than hardcoded so the dev origin
# and the GitHub Pages origin can use different ones.
#
# Getting one is a few minutes in the Google Cloud Console: create a project, enable
# the Google Drive API, set the OAuth consent screen to External and leave it in
# Testing, add your own account as a test user, then create an OAuth client of type
# "Web application". The app signs in with the GIS popup token flow, which uses
# authorised JavaScript **origins** only — `http://localhost:5173` for dev, plus your
# Pages origin to deploy. No redirect URI is required.
#
# Only the `drive.file` scope is used, and it stays that way: the broader Drive scopes
# are restricted and would need a Google security assessment. The consequence is worth
# knowing before you swap this value — the app can only ever see files its own client
# created, so a new Client ID means the previous account's clips and `loops.json` go
# invisible rather than merely inaccessible. Re-uploading is expected, not a bug.
#
# The real value has the shape `<project-number>-<hash>.apps.googleusercontent.com`.
# The placeholder below deliberately does NOT: anything ending in that suffix is
# taken for a real Client ID and sent to Google, which rejects it as
# `invalid_client` — and a rejection is indistinguishable from the dancer
# declining consent, so an unedited copy of this file would accuse them of
# refusing access they were never asked for.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=paste-your-client-id-here
